%I A136541 #15 Oct 28 2026 00:11:16
%S A136541 33,2889,235953,19129689,823564514029689
%N A136541 Numbers k such that sum of the proper divisors of k is equal to (3/4)*phi(k).
%C A136541 If m>0 and p=4*3^m-1 is prime (m is in the sequence A005540), then k=3^m*p is in the sequence since sigma(k)-k = (1/2)*(3^(m+1)-1)*4*3^m-3^m*(4*3^m-1) = 3^m*(2*3^m-1) = (3/4)*(2*3^(m-1))*((4*3^m-1)-1) = (3/4)*phi(3^m)*phi(p) = (3/4)*phi(3^m*p) =(3/4)*phi(k). The first five terms of the sequence are of such form. Is it true that all terms are of the mentioned form?
%C A136541 Any term not of the form 3^m*(4*3^m-1) must be an odd square. a(6) > 10^36. - _Max Alekseyev_, Oct 15 2026
%F A136541 For n=1,2,3 & 4 a(n)=3^(2n-1)*(4*3^(2n-1)-1).
%e A136541 sigma(33)-33=48-33=15=(3/4)*20=(3/4)*phi(33).
%t A136541 Do[If[DivisorSigma[1,n]-n==3/4*EulerPhi@n,Print[n]],{n,2000000000}]
%Y A136541 Cf. A005540, A076373.
%K A136541 more,nonn,changed
%O A136541 1,1
